1
00:00:02,068 --> 00:00:13,355
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I've got a passage from a sutta, Majjhima Nikaya, 48, Kosambi Sutta.

2
00:00:13,535 --> 00:00:16,196
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the end of it, the last few paragraphs.

3
00:00:17,197 --> 00:00:22,920
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought it was quite a nice description of the understanding of the Sotāpanna.

4
00:00:23,901 --> 00:00:28,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I thought it might be something to talk about.

5
00:00:30,744 --> 00:00:31,045
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

6
00:00:34,125 --> 00:00:45,271
[SPEAKER_00]: It's after the bit where the Buddha's talking about what leads to friendliness, cordiality, and bhikkhus, and saṅgha.

7
00:00:45,812 --> 00:00:57,939
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says these six things lead to... if, you know, engaging these six things, the saṅgha will be harmonious, unity, living in concord.

8
00:00:58,479 --> 00:01:01,801
[SPEAKER_00]: And the last one is having the right view, sharing the right view.

9
00:01:03,325 --> 00:01:08,948
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says that's the peak, that's the most... That's the fundamental one.

10
00:01:08,968 --> 00:01:10,448
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the fundamental one, yeah.

11
00:01:11,149 --> 00:01:12,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else is sort of secondary to that.

12
00:01:13,050 --> 00:01:24,335
[SPEAKER_00]: So then he says, and how, Bhikkhus, does this view, which is noble, leading on, lead whoever acts on this, on, towards the correct destruction of suffering?

13
00:01:25,877 --> 00:01:32,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Here bhikkhus, a bhikkhu gone to the forest, or gone to the root of a tree, or gone to an empty dwelling, reflects thus.

14
00:01:33,842 --> 00:01:43,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any internal obsession unabandoned by me, because of which, with this obsession, I can't understand, I can't see the essence of the obsessed mind?

15
00:01:46,170 --> 00:01:52,595
[SPEAKER_00]: If bhikkhus, a bhikkhu is obsessed by the passion of sense desire, there's just an obsessed mind.

16
00:01:53,672 --> 00:01:58,756
[SPEAKER_00]: If Bhikkhu is obsessed by ill-will, there is just an obsessed mind.

17
00:01:58,776 --> 00:02:08,543
[SPEAKER_00]: If a Bhikkhu is obsessed by sloth and torpor, tiredness and sluggishness, there is just an obsessed mind.

18
00:02:08,663 --> 00:02:13,627
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's obsessed by agitation and remorse, it's just an obsessed mind.

19
00:02:14,823 --> 00:02:17,185
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's obsessed by doubt, there's just an obsessed mind.

20
00:02:17,385 --> 00:02:20,887
[SPEAKER_00]: If Abhikkhu is engaged in thoughts about this world, it's just an obsessed mind.

21
00:02:21,507 --> 00:02:25,049
[SPEAKER_00]: If Abhikkhu is engaged in thoughts about another world, it's just an obsessed mind.

22
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:36,276
[SPEAKER_00]: If Abhikkhu is involved in quarrels, disputes, having fallen into an argument, dwells stabbing others with verbal daggers, there is just an obsessed mind.

23
00:02:36,296 --> 00:02:38,418
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands in this way.

24
00:02:38,478 --> 00:02:41,800
[SPEAKER_00]: There isn't any internal obsession, unabandoned by me,

25
00:02:42,766 --> 00:02:49,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of which, this obsession, with this obsession, I can't understand, I can't see the essence of the obsessed mind.

26
00:02:50,152 --> 00:02:53,594
[SPEAKER_00]: My mind is well-directed for the awakening of truths, of the truths.

27
00:02:54,475 --> 00:03:00,279
[SPEAKER_00]: For him, this first knowledge is attained, which is noble, world-transcendent, not shared by cotuginous.

28
00:03:06,884 --> 00:03:07,584
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the first one.

29
00:03:08,065 --> 00:03:09,626
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what all those things have in common?

30
00:03:12,176 --> 00:03:13,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Things as in of the obsessions?

31
00:03:14,539 --> 00:03:22,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, all the... doubt is an obsession, your world is an obsession, charity is an obsession, thinking about this world is an obsession, the other world is an obsession.

32
00:03:23,111 --> 00:03:25,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's all mind.

33
00:03:26,195 --> 00:03:27,277
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all mind, exactly.

34
00:03:27,798 --> 00:03:27,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

35
00:03:29,577 --> 00:03:38,199
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not... and that's... that's... so, it says there clearly, it's not... he chases down and analyzes doubt, chases down, analyzes centrality.

36
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:41,981
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he just knows this results in obsession of the mind.

37
00:03:42,001 --> 00:03:43,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, obsession is there.

38
00:03:43,181 --> 00:03:45,321
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows obsession as obsession being there.

39
00:03:46,082 --> 00:03:49,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means understanding of the nature of the mind.

40
00:03:50,603 --> 00:03:52,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Nature of it, not the content of it.

41
00:03:53,164 --> 00:03:53,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

42
00:03:54,284 --> 00:03:55,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the experience of it.

43
00:03:55,664 --> 00:03:57,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Why this special experience, this...

44
00:03:58,064 --> 00:04:07,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Out-of-this-world experience, not-out-of-this-world experience, experience-of, free-from-lust, with-lust, all of that, is secondary to the mind being first, in that sense.

45
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:09,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

46
00:04:09,401 --> 00:04:13,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding the nature of that mind means surmounting anything that can obsess that mind.

47
00:04:13,987 --> 00:04:14,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

48
00:04:15,322 --> 00:04:18,845
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he talks there about these different obsessions.

49
00:04:19,625 --> 00:04:24,689
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is that for each one, this is just an example of an obsessed mind.

50
00:04:24,969 --> 00:04:27,651
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what they have in common that's the most important thing.

51
00:04:27,711 --> 00:04:28,852
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

52
00:04:29,533 --> 00:04:32,315
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop trying to discern and analyze the content.

53
00:04:32,595 --> 00:04:37,879
[SPEAKER_01]: You just see it as an example of the nature behind it that you want to discern.

54
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:43,603
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, only then will you know, will you be able to answer for yourself.

55
00:04:44,759 --> 00:04:46,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Right now, there is an obsessed mind.

56
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:48,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

57
00:04:48,161 --> 00:04:48,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you wouldn't know.

58
00:04:48,861 --> 00:04:52,303
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know what an obsessed mind is... Well, otherwise, it's not up to you.

59
00:04:52,503 --> 00:04:59,988
[SPEAKER_01]: If your obsessed mind is determined by presence of greed, aversion, delusion, or thoughts of this world, it means you will be affected when those things are there.

60
00:05:01,108 --> 00:05:07,332
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you develop your mind so when obsession of... when ill-will is present, the mind is not obsessed by it.

61
00:05:08,352 --> 00:05:09,333
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ill-will is still there.

62
00:05:09,373 --> 00:05:10,713
[SPEAKER_01]: The content is still the same.

63
00:05:10,834 --> 00:05:11,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

64
00:05:11,674 --> 00:05:13,275
[SPEAKER_01]: But the mind is now not obsessed.

65
00:05:14,334 --> 00:05:26,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you've understood that whether there's an obsessed mind or an unobsessed mind... Because, basically, you refrain from entertaining the further obsession towards the content and you force yourself to look for the nature of it.

66
00:05:28,301 --> 00:05:32,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop chasing down the individual experiences and start understanding them universally.

67
00:05:33,564 --> 00:05:34,344
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

68
00:05:35,505 --> 00:05:39,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Because obsession does not... it requires maintenance.

69
00:05:39,747 --> 00:05:40,968
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires your involvement.

70
00:05:43,713 --> 00:05:45,973
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it requires you to choose, basically, to feed it.

71
00:05:46,734 --> 00:05:46,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

72
00:05:47,554 --> 00:05:59,236
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like when people know, like, you know that, or at least you have the idea, that suffering is caused by craving, not by adverse circumstances you experience in the world.

73
00:06:00,116 --> 00:06:10,798
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you experience the adverse, like, say, somebody comes and says something upsetting to you, so you know that on a theoretical level, you suffer because of your craving, not because of what they said.

74
00:06:11,925 --> 00:06:13,846
[SPEAKER_01]: But on practical level, you can't restrain yourself.

75
00:06:14,947 --> 00:06:17,249
[SPEAKER_00]: And you still go after that person or whatever.

76
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:24,114
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot help yourself but be obsessed about the content that upsets you.

77
00:06:25,295 --> 00:06:27,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, same when you meet agreeable experience.

78
00:06:28,657 --> 00:06:35,442
[SPEAKER_01]: The prospect of sensuality, you know, the excitement, you are not developed in regard to it, you cannot help but be obsessed.

79
00:06:36,323 --> 00:06:37,524
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you choose to give in.

80
00:06:38,449 --> 00:06:51,637
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, that's why, like we... in so many talks, like, sense restraint is not optional.

81
00:06:51,957 --> 00:06:53,018
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be a sotāpanna?

82
00:06:53,058 --> 00:07:02,724
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to develop that sense restraint to the point that you are able to see the nature of whatever's presently in front of you, in terms of the content of your experience,

83
00:07:03,686 --> 00:07:09,329
[SPEAKER_01]: And not be obsessed by it, and not go after it, and not be pulled by saying yes, or repelled by saying no.

84
00:07:09,529 --> 00:07:18,353
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sense-restraint, another way of sort of describing sense-restraint, would be refusing, not acting out of obsession.

85
00:07:18,373 --> 00:07:19,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of obsession.

86
00:07:19,554 --> 00:07:21,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Good obsession or bad obsession, doesn't matter.

87
00:07:21,535 --> 00:07:22,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Obsession is there.

88
00:07:24,716 --> 00:07:26,457
[SPEAKER_00]: And in order to do that...

89
00:07:26,497 --> 00:07:28,478
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sense-restraint outlines your mind.

90
00:07:28,978 --> 00:07:29,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

91
00:07:29,639 --> 00:07:32,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Which then gets you to discern what obsession actually is.

92
00:07:32,965 --> 00:07:33,745
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I was going to say.

93
00:07:33,765 --> 00:07:38,167
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems to be like you're talking about cittanimitta, seeing... Yeah, the sign of the mind.

94
00:07:38,227 --> 00:07:38,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

95
00:07:38,887 --> 00:07:45,049
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the necessary, as the Buddha said, prerequisite for sotāpati, for the right view, is to develop sign of your mind.

96
00:07:45,329 --> 00:07:46,150
[SPEAKER_01]: To see the mind.

97
00:07:46,230 --> 00:07:57,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means able to, like, transcend the presently enduring content that's pulling you, pushing you, upsetting you, obsessing you, and you still see beyond it, in terms of the mind that endures there simultaneously.

98
00:07:57,493 --> 00:07:58,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, which is what...

99
00:07:59,485 --> 00:08:02,186
[SPEAKER_00]: All of these different types of mind have in common.

100
00:08:02,606 --> 00:08:17,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, exactly, exactly.

101
00:08:17,913 --> 00:08:28,458
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's, like, it just seems to be that's one, that's one knowledge that, eh, it says, like, a sotāpanna can ask himself, he ponders, he thinks, okay, can I

102
00:08:29,934 --> 00:08:36,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I aware of the presence of obsessed mind or non-obsessed mind?

103
00:08:36,099 --> 00:08:36,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

104
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:52,433
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how, by being unable to be obsessed, even amidst obsessing circumstances, obsessing content, that's how you become unable to suffer, even amidst suffering, as Buddha said, which begins with sotāpanna.

105
00:08:53,814 --> 00:08:55,196
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you ask yourself,

106
00:08:56,584 --> 00:08:58,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I free from suffering in that sense?

107
00:08:58,325 --> 00:09:02,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I not suffer even while I'm experiencing that which is suffering, that which is disagreeable?

108
00:09:03,528 --> 00:09:05,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Would my mind remain unobsessed by it?

109
00:09:06,129 --> 00:09:07,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Or would I fear it?

110
00:09:07,129 --> 00:09:08,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I incline away from it?

111
00:09:08,570 --> 00:09:09,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I try to shelter myself?

112
00:09:09,851 --> 00:09:10,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I try to protect myself?

113
00:09:11,391 --> 00:09:16,654
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those attitudes still imply that you think suffering is there, so you're protecting yourself from it.

114
00:09:16,774 --> 00:09:17,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

115
00:09:17,935 --> 00:09:22,057
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't think it would... a practitioner wouldn't be able to conceive of that question being asked.

116
00:09:22,771 --> 00:09:26,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if transparent, Patlujana probably could.

117
00:09:27,154 --> 00:09:32,257
[SPEAKER_01]: The one that's not avoiding responsibility or mystifying the content of his experience.

118
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:37,360
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point.

119
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:38,080
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

120
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:45,205
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, it's not only... this doesn't only refer to things that are unpleasant in their nature, that obsess your mind.

121
00:09:45,325 --> 00:09:46,386
[SPEAKER_00]: It refers to things that are pleasant.

122
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:53,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Chasing novelty experience, chasing those meditation releases, all of that is focused on the content of it.

123
00:09:53,881 --> 00:09:54,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

124
00:09:54,121 --> 00:09:55,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you're completely unaware of the nature.

125
00:09:55,722 --> 00:09:57,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Unaware of the nature means unaware of the mind.

126
00:09:57,482 --> 00:09:57,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

127
00:09:57,962 --> 00:10:00,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Unaware of the mind, there is no prerequisite for the right view.

128
00:10:00,663 --> 00:10:00,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

129
00:10:00,883 --> 00:10:02,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how special your experience might be.

130
00:10:04,984 --> 00:10:08,145
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I was saying before, like that whole, like eh...

131
00:10:09,180 --> 00:10:13,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Overly affirming or overly denying attitude towards the content of your experience.

132
00:10:13,781 --> 00:10:23,562
[SPEAKER_01]: And same when we mention in our talks when people have mystical experience and it's really, it's not the experience that's mystical, it's their attitude that mystifies it.

133
00:10:24,623 --> 00:10:28,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Any experience is just an experience, just the content of the senses or the mind as well.

134
00:10:29,043 --> 00:10:29,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

135
00:10:29,784 --> 00:10:34,644
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's your attitude whether mystifies, whether glorifies and values the content in itself.

136
00:10:35,004 --> 00:10:35,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

137
00:10:35,565 --> 00:10:36,865
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's mystical.

138
00:10:36,945 --> 00:10:38,105
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a wrong view.

139
00:10:39,387 --> 00:10:39,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

140
00:10:41,688 --> 00:10:51,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Not what you... but if... see, for as long as you value what you experience or what you want or what you might, to that extent, you value the view of the content of your experience.

141
00:10:51,294 --> 00:10:51,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

142
00:10:51,734 --> 00:10:52,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the nature of it.

143
00:10:53,235 --> 00:10:54,996
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't matter what you experience.

144
00:10:55,376 --> 00:10:55,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

145
00:10:55,877 --> 00:10:57,838
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is whether the mind is obsessed by it or not.

146
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:03,321
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can only...

147
00:11:04,702 --> 00:11:07,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Not be obsessed by it, or affected by it.

148
00:11:07,583 --> 00:11:08,243
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing the nature.

149
00:11:08,263 --> 00:11:08,963
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing the nature.

150
00:11:08,983 --> 00:11:09,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

151
00:11:09,963 --> 00:11:13,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by learning how to deal with content perfectly.

152
00:11:14,005 --> 00:11:14,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

153
00:11:14,245 --> 00:11:14,945
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

154
00:11:18,246 --> 00:11:26,388
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you don't want to be free from suffering, you will be free from suffering, because that's the automatic result of seeing the nature of things.

155
00:11:26,548 --> 00:11:26,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

156
00:11:26,748 --> 00:11:29,349
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot suffer, because suffering requires an obsession.

157
00:11:29,549 --> 00:11:29,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

158
00:11:30,209 --> 00:11:31,489
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to then do something.

159
00:11:31,590 --> 00:11:31,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

160
00:11:36,755 --> 00:11:40,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Furthermore, bhikkhus, a noble disciple reflects thus.

161
00:11:40,418 --> 00:11:44,200
[SPEAKER_00]: It's funny that in the previous one, he just said a bhikkhu.

162
00:11:45,841 --> 00:11:47,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, bhikkhu is a bhikkhu gone to the forest.

163
00:11:48,063 --> 00:11:53,126
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, the rest of these samajas are all about a noble disciple.

164
00:11:53,346 --> 00:11:54,427
[SPEAKER_00]: He reflects thus.

165
00:11:55,148 --> 00:12:01,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Pursuing, developing, making much of this view, do I gain calm, samatha, for myself?

166
00:12:03,133 --> 00:12:05,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I gain extinguishment of myself?

167
00:12:07,932 --> 00:12:08,993
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands in this way.

168
00:12:09,113 --> 00:12:14,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Pursuing, developing, making much of this view, I gain calm for myself.

169
00:12:14,619 --> 00:12:17,621
[SPEAKER_00]: I gain extinguishment of myself.

170
00:12:17,642 --> 00:12:18,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbhuti.

171
00:12:18,522 --> 00:12:20,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

172
00:12:20,064 --> 00:12:23,707
[SPEAKER_01]: He's just further verifying exactly what you already know.

173
00:12:25,289 --> 00:12:29,013
[SPEAKER_01]: You already know that this view, this way of regarding things,

174
00:12:30,196 --> 00:12:34,741
[SPEAKER_01]: It frees you from obsessions, which implies inability to suffer.

175
00:12:35,501 --> 00:12:46,192
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I pursue that view, oh, then the further calm comes out of it, and then the further extinguishment of anything else, in terms of views and habits that were rooted upon greed, aversion and delusion.

176
00:12:51,427 --> 00:12:52,347
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the second knowledge.

177
00:12:52,608 --> 00:12:55,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, furthermore, a noble disciple reflects thus.

178
00:12:56,009 --> 00:13:03,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as I'm endowed with this view, are there any other ascetics or brahmins outside here endowed with such a view?

179
00:13:04,514 --> 00:13:05,434
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands in this way.

180
00:13:05,554 --> 00:13:05,694
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

181
00:13:06,034 --> 00:13:10,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as I'm endowed with this view, there aren't any other ascetics and brahmins outside here endowed with this view.

182
00:13:11,077 --> 00:13:16,400
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to arrive at that view is to understand it correctly, which is only found within the Buddha's instruction.

183
00:13:17,420 --> 00:13:18,120
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

184
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:18,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And see what that says?

185
00:13:19,541 --> 00:13:20,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what that implies is

186
00:13:21,420 --> 00:13:24,362
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible to stumble upon it spontaneously.

187
00:13:24,382 --> 00:13:24,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

188
00:13:25,463 --> 00:13:27,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, eh... accidentally.

189
00:13:27,545 --> 00:13:27,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

190
00:13:28,566 --> 00:13:33,510
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even when, like the... before the Buddha, like Pacheco Buddha, even they don't stumble upon it spontaneously.

191
00:13:34,311 --> 00:13:37,833
[SPEAKER_01]: They recognize the value of their development, they pursue it, and they free their mind.

192
00:13:37,853 --> 00:13:37,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

193
00:13:38,014 --> 00:13:39,735
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense that Sammasambuddha did.

194
00:13:40,155 --> 00:13:40,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

195
00:13:40,876 --> 00:13:50,644
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's another, eh... sort of, eh... clear indication of how you... why you should not be chasing your... your experiences.

196
00:13:51,110 --> 00:13:55,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if a spiritual nature... Because they're just... Because they happen to you.

197
00:13:55,172 --> 00:13:55,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

198
00:13:55,412 --> 00:13:56,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding doesn't happen to you.

199
00:13:56,933 --> 00:13:59,314
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop it in regard to things that happen to you.

200
00:13:59,974 --> 00:14:04,116
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you value things happening to you, you will not be understanding it.

201
00:14:04,256 --> 00:14:05,337
[SPEAKER_01]: It's mutually exclusive.

202
00:14:05,357 --> 00:14:10,379
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either in the content or you're refraining from valuing the content and looking at the nature of it.

203
00:14:18,722 --> 00:14:20,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Furthermore, Anobha Dissalpa reflects thus.

204
00:14:21,805 --> 00:14:32,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as an individual who has attained the right view is endowed with a nature, usually it's like with a character, with a body, you know, he has a particular nature.

205
00:14:32,614 --> 00:14:34,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I endowed with such a nature?

206
00:14:34,815 --> 00:14:39,980
[SPEAKER_00]: In what way, and in what way because an individual who has attained the right view endowed with such a nature?

207
00:14:41,182 --> 00:14:43,824
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the nature of an individual who has attained the right view.

208
00:14:44,224 --> 00:14:54,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though such a one falls into an offense, such that the rehabilitation from that offense is known, still he quickly indicates, discloses, clarifies to a teacher or to his wise companions in the holy life.

209
00:14:54,731 --> 00:14:58,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Having indicated, disclosed, clarified, he undergoes restraint in the future.

210
00:14:58,654 --> 00:14:59,474
[SPEAKER_01]: And what does that mean?

211
00:15:00,255 --> 00:15:08,240
[SPEAKER_01]: That means that the utmost personal things, personal wrongdoings, cannot be taken as personal anymore.

212
00:15:08,873 --> 00:15:09,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, yeah.

213
00:15:09,654 --> 00:15:10,514
[SPEAKER_01]: He discloses it.

214
00:15:10,554 --> 00:15:14,778
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason people don't disclose things is because of the involvement of their conceit.

215
00:15:16,499 --> 00:15:16,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Kind of.

216
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:17,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, whatever.

217
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:20,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear or whatever, but it's all rooted in sense of I am in regard to it.

218
00:15:21,103 --> 00:15:21,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Self-view.

219
00:15:22,804 --> 00:15:25,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Self-worth that comes out of the self-view.

220
00:15:25,366 --> 00:15:25,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

221
00:15:26,207 --> 00:15:34,654
[SPEAKER_01]: And for somebody with the right view, it's... he's incapable of taking it personally, even things that resulted like wrongdoings out of still remnants of his personality.

222
00:15:36,075 --> 00:15:36,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

223
00:15:36,315 --> 00:15:38,137
[SPEAKER_01]: His greed, his aversion, his delusion.

224
00:15:38,217 --> 00:15:38,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

225
00:15:38,993 --> 00:15:45,699
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's unable to take it personally, on that fundamental level, to not disclose it, or not see it as a... oh, that was a wrong doing.

226
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:47,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

227
00:15:47,060 --> 00:15:50,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's good.

228
00:15:50,543 --> 00:15:56,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you... if you... if you recognize you've done something wrong, and then you have this...

229
00:15:56,127 --> 00:15:57,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of looking for excuses.

230
00:15:57,388 --> 00:16:01,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you start covering up, start making it opaque and non-transparent.

231
00:16:01,272 --> 00:16:01,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

232
00:16:02,286 --> 00:16:03,006
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

233
00:16:03,527 --> 00:16:13,738
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's impossible for a person with the right view, layperson or a monk, doesn't matter, to become opaque again and lie to himself about his own motives or doings or actions.

234
00:16:14,799 --> 00:16:15,420
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

235
00:16:20,188 --> 00:16:21,909
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, there's the simile basis.

236
00:16:22,309 --> 00:16:35,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Just because, just as a little young boy, a baby, having stepped on burning coal with his hand or foot, quickly moves back, in just this way, because this is the nature of an individual who's attained right view.

237
00:16:35,397 --> 00:16:39,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though he falls into a fancy quickly, discloses, indicates, sort of immediately.

238
00:16:39,820 --> 00:16:40,800
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the first reaction.

239
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:41,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

240
00:16:41,381 --> 00:16:45,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's nothing getting in the way of him, you know...

241
00:16:46,924 --> 00:16:47,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Covering it up.

242
00:16:47,464 --> 00:16:48,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Covering it up, yeah.

243
00:16:50,592 --> 00:16:52,774
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for him, this is the fourth knowledge.

244
00:16:53,715 --> 00:16:59,481
[SPEAKER_00]: This fourth knowledge is attained, which is noble, beyond the world, shared, not shared by patujanas.

245
00:17:01,843 --> 00:17:04,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Furthermore, bhikkhus, a noble disciple reflects thus.

246
00:17:05,386 --> 00:17:10,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as an individual who's attained the right view is endowed with a nature, am I endowed with such a nature?

247
00:17:10,371 --> 00:17:11,713
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's the same, same thing.

248
00:17:13,624 --> 00:17:19,210
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, eh... Bhikkhus, this is the nature of an individual who has attained the right view.

249
00:17:20,070 --> 00:17:33,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though he has become active in terms of various duties with his companions in the holy life, there's still a keen regard for training in the higher virtue, for training in the higher mind, for training in the higher understanding.

250
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:49,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhus, just as a cow eats the grass with her young calf, and she watches over her young calf, in just this way, bhikkhus, this is the nature of every individual who's taken the right view, even though he has become active, in terms of various duties, he doesn't give up training.

251
00:17:49,910 --> 00:17:55,534
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't lose the context, despite the other more mundane things he might have to do.

252
00:17:56,615 --> 00:17:57,315
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it means.

253
00:17:58,476 --> 00:18:03,860
[SPEAKER_01]: We cannot forget the context of the right view, and the implicit development in it, and so on.

254
00:18:03,980 --> 00:18:04,400
[SPEAKER_01]: And the very

255
00:18:05,815 --> 00:18:07,195
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be ignored.

256
00:18:07,215 --> 00:18:15,738
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even like back in the suttas, where lay people became sotāpānas and then kind of chose to continue living their household life.

257
00:18:16,738 --> 00:18:17,878
[SPEAKER_01]: That's something they cannot forget.

258
00:18:18,678 --> 00:18:18,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

259
00:18:19,038 --> 00:18:24,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, throughout their life, they always start... but basically, after the right view, they would have felt

260
00:18:25,877 --> 00:18:30,818
[SPEAKER_01]: At least a degree of discrepancy between the value of the right view and development and what they're doing.

261
00:18:30,958 --> 00:18:32,318
[SPEAKER_00]: It's also an obstacle, isn't it?

262
00:18:32,338 --> 00:18:32,819
[SPEAKER_00]: It says that.

263
00:18:32,859 --> 00:18:34,319
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an obstacle in that's in Udana.

264
00:18:34,339 --> 00:18:35,599
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha says...

265
00:18:35,639 --> 00:18:44,921
[SPEAKER_01]: I think in Udana it says the noble disciple will sort of not progress because fondness for work, talk and sleep.

266
00:18:44,941 --> 00:18:46,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's in the aggregate kind.

267
00:18:46,301 --> 00:18:48,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, yeah, maybe I'm wrong.

268
00:18:48,282 --> 00:18:48,762
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

269
00:18:48,782 --> 00:18:49,262
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is

270
00:18:50,037 --> 00:18:53,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Work, yeah, will... an obstacle for a noble disciple.

271
00:18:53,339 --> 00:18:59,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and why, like, in other sutras, like, you might wonder, well, how would somebody who's so dependent not pursue further?

272
00:18:59,103 --> 00:19:01,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't feel you don't have... you don't feel like you have to.

273
00:19:02,385 --> 00:19:05,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Other people might be, like, some people might be satisfied with that.

274
00:19:06,027 --> 00:19:11,551
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you cannot suffer amid suffering, and everything else is literally seven grains of sand left.

275
00:19:12,191 --> 00:19:16,334
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know, you know that there are mountains or Himalayas, that's how much you've done already.

276
00:19:17,162 --> 00:19:19,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, oh, this is nothing.

277
00:19:19,184 --> 00:19:20,985
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's nothing from that point of view.

278
00:19:21,045 --> 00:19:23,887
[SPEAKER_01]: But they would have said, even that little is still something, so that's why you should do it.

279
00:19:24,308 --> 00:19:25,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

280
00:19:25,268 --> 00:19:37,478
[SPEAKER_01]: But some monks or lay people would have just been content with that and which would have allowed them to live the life which cannot be aligned with the right view.

281
00:19:37,902 --> 00:19:38,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

282
00:19:38,162 --> 00:19:39,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Lay life cannot be aligned with the right view.

283
00:19:40,122 --> 00:19:40,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

284
00:19:40,843 --> 00:19:46,304
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you chose to stay a layman or as a bhikkhu, sometimes, you chose to basically not really practice.

285
00:19:46,424 --> 00:19:46,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

286
00:19:47,124 --> 00:19:49,704
[SPEAKER_01]: That's against the right view and you have to live that discrepancy.

287
00:19:49,724 --> 00:19:51,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Live like a layman in clothes.

288
00:19:51,485 --> 00:19:51,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

289
00:19:51,905 --> 00:19:57,366
[SPEAKER_01]: You would feel that discrepancy and sooner or later, you would have to basically... you would turn away from it.

290
00:19:57,486 --> 00:19:57,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

291
00:19:58,166 --> 00:19:58,926
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you're saying.

292
00:19:58,946 --> 00:20:00,046
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you're saying here is...

293
00:20:00,727 --> 00:20:02,387
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you're doing that... Hmm.

294
00:20:04,418 --> 00:20:07,499
[SPEAKER_00]: As a noble disciple, you can't forget.

295
00:20:07,559 --> 00:20:08,119
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot.

296
00:20:08,199 --> 00:20:13,281
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's like the right view inclines towards nibbāna, slopes towards... that's its natural direction.

297
00:20:13,521 --> 00:20:16,782
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... now you've established that, and you cannot not incline that way.

298
00:20:17,563 --> 00:20:23,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And it says, at most, seven lifetimes, at most, you will have to go in nibbāna by the end of it.

299
00:20:24,625 --> 00:20:29,527
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is another, again, another... And that's the only reason, by the way, why Sotāpanna would sometimes take seven lifetimes.

300
00:20:30,171 --> 00:20:30,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're not.

301
00:20:31,152 --> 00:20:32,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're not practicing.

302
00:20:32,513 --> 00:20:32,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

303
00:20:33,174 --> 00:20:42,222
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even with the utmost non-practice, within seven items, you would have to basically drain any interest towards anything that's that's discrepant with the right view.

304
00:20:42,982 --> 00:20:44,663
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not compatible.

305
00:20:44,963 --> 00:20:45,724
[SPEAKER_01]: You would abandon it.

306
00:20:46,144 --> 00:20:58,011
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if you're not practicing, and you're not behaving, you're behaving in a way that's sort of contradicting your understanding, you still understand, you still have this knowledge, you still... Oh, yeah, that's... you cannot lose that.

307
00:20:58,031 --> 00:21:00,853
[SPEAKER_00]: You reflect, you go... Because... and you know why?

308
00:21:00,913 --> 00:21:06,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not given to you like a random special experience that happened and you don't know how or why.

309
00:21:06,257 --> 00:21:06,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

310
00:21:06,957 --> 00:21:10,859
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you understood the nature of anything that arises for you.

311
00:21:11,199 --> 00:21:15,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, whatever has the nature of arising, that's why that nature is impermanent.

312
00:21:15,901 --> 00:21:18,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the always the the inside of sotāpanna and the suttas.

313
00:21:19,923 --> 00:21:23,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Not when this special thing arose for me, I became Sotāpanna.

314
00:21:23,486 --> 00:21:23,886
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

315
00:21:24,186 --> 00:21:30,711
[SPEAKER_01]: It's understanding that whatever is of a nature to manifest, that's why it cannot be mine, because it has manifested.

316
00:21:31,071 --> 00:21:36,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Like in this case, it began with the nature of the mind, recognizing the nature of the mind.

317
00:21:36,575 --> 00:21:41,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and that understanding of that manifestation is on the level of the mind, yeah, not on the level of the content.

318
00:21:46,314 --> 00:21:48,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Furthermore, a noble disciple reflects thus.

319
00:21:48,675 --> 00:21:49,616
[SPEAKER_00]: I just quite like this.

320
00:21:49,656 --> 00:21:59,020
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just these, you know, it's about these things that the noble disciple thinks about.

321
00:22:00,841 --> 00:22:04,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as an individual who has attained the right view is endowed with strength,

322
00:22:05,362 --> 00:22:06,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I endowed with such strength?

323
00:22:07,163 --> 00:22:12,365
[SPEAKER_00]: And in what way bhikkhus is an individual who has attained the right view endowed with such strength?

324
00:22:13,126 --> 00:22:15,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhus, this is the strength of an individual who has attained the right view.

325
00:22:16,007 --> 00:22:27,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has been made known by the tathāgata in this dhamma and discipline, while it's being taught, having heeded the meaning, having attended, having collected together with the whole mind, lending an ear, he listens to the dhamma.

326
00:22:30,994 --> 00:22:33,315
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands in this way, just as an individual has attained.

327
00:22:33,335 --> 00:22:33,675
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

328
00:22:35,326 --> 00:22:36,406
[SPEAKER_00]: He listens to the dhamma.

329
00:22:36,466 --> 00:22:46,968
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't like... Well, he cannot... it doesn't take effort for him to listen to the dhamma, because his experience now has been aligned with the universal principle of the dhamma, through his understanding.

330
00:22:47,589 --> 00:22:53,790
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever he comes across the dhamma, it's immediately that strength is there, that direct connection.

331
00:22:53,870 --> 00:22:55,290
[SPEAKER_01]: And even that is not personal.

332
00:22:55,590 --> 00:22:56,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

333
00:22:56,050 --> 00:22:58,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, let me make the effort and think about the dhamma.

334
00:22:58,331 --> 00:23:04,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when he listens to the dhamma, it's basically just he gets reminded of what's already within his experience seen.

335
00:23:10,405 --> 00:23:13,446
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, again, the next one's similar.

336
00:23:13,486 --> 00:23:18,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as an individual who has attained the right view is endowed with strength, am I endowed with such strength?

337
00:23:18,147 --> 00:23:18,967
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the strength?

338
00:23:18,987 --> 00:23:22,347
[SPEAKER_00]: The strength of an individual who has attained right view is this.

339
00:23:22,667 --> 00:23:35,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has been made known by the Tathāgata in this dhamma discipline, while it's being taught, he... so I've translated this as he gets an experience of the meaning.

340
00:23:36,764 --> 00:23:40,428
[SPEAKER_00]: He gets an experience of the dhamma.

341
00:23:40,488 --> 00:23:42,791
[SPEAKER_00]: He attains joy associated with the dhamma.

342
00:23:42,851 --> 00:23:50,940
[SPEAKER_01]: When it's being said, even if he doesn't think about the dhamma, what's being heard is what he experiences as a direct description of what he is.

343
00:23:53,052 --> 00:24:03,717
[SPEAKER_01]: That's also why a Sotāpanna will be able to tell whether somebody has the right view or not, or whether somebody talks... what somebody said is aligned with the right view or not, even if it's not coming from the Buddha.

344
00:24:04,477 --> 00:24:10,280
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody makes a statement, he will be able to tell whether that statement is accurate or not, whether it's true or false.

345
00:24:10,380 --> 00:24:11,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Based on the experience.

346
00:24:11,381 --> 00:24:14,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Based on the direct experience of the meaning of that statement.

347
00:24:16,516 --> 00:24:22,923
[SPEAKER_00]: The Pali there is Atavada and Atavada and Dhammavada... Ataveda and Dhammaveda.

348
00:24:23,363 --> 00:24:27,548
[SPEAKER_00]: And usually it's translated as kind of some spirit... religious joy.

349
00:24:28,028 --> 00:24:28,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right, right.

350
00:24:28,789 --> 00:24:31,492
[SPEAKER_01]: In the... No, it's more like a universal joy.

351
00:24:32,094 --> 00:24:38,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I mean, it might be joy, but it... You know, like, when you understood something for yourself, and then you see it in the world understood by others.

352
00:24:38,475 --> 00:24:39,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, yeah, yeah.

353
00:24:39,215 --> 00:24:41,556
[SPEAKER_01]: That already provides you with joy, even on a mundane level.

354
00:24:41,576 --> 00:24:42,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

355
00:24:42,056 --> 00:24:44,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone on a level of freedom from suffering.

356
00:24:44,216 --> 00:24:44,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

357
00:24:44,397 --> 00:24:48,377
[SPEAKER_01]: And somebody talks about freedom from suffering, you cannot not be joyous about it.

358
00:24:48,397 --> 00:24:48,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

359
00:24:48,897 --> 00:24:51,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's universal, it's beyond you, in that sense.

360
00:24:51,418 --> 00:24:52,798
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be taken personally.

361
00:24:53,438 --> 00:24:56,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, even the joy you get from it, it's beyond your personality.

362
00:24:58,960 --> 00:25:00,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why it's a joy that's

363
00:25:02,180 --> 00:25:03,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to be feared, and it's to be cultivated.

364
00:25:03,761 --> 00:25:07,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing wrong with that joy, yeah.

365
00:25:09,506 --> 00:25:14,249
[SPEAKER_01]: No, like, people get... experience joy when they find somebody else sharing their central preference.

366
00:25:16,011 --> 00:25:19,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, saying tasty music, or whatever, they get that... Wow!

367
00:25:19,713 --> 00:25:20,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

368
00:25:20,494 --> 00:25:22,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine... Oh, yeah, that band's great, isn't it?

369
00:25:22,035 --> 00:25:22,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

370
00:25:22,776 --> 00:25:28,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine how much more, when you find somebody also talking about freedom from suffering, exactly how it is.

371
00:25:28,140 --> 00:25:28,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yes.

372
00:25:32,588 --> 00:25:35,512
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's a joy you can't crave for.

373
00:25:35,532 --> 00:25:43,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Because craving requires obsession, requires personality, requires basically lack of that sight of your own mind.

